EDTALK HISTORY
--------------
EdTalk V0.98g -- 17 July 1993

This is an intermediate release. I haven't had a chance to work on EdTalk
for the past month or two, and since it looks like it will be another few
weeks before I can get back to it, I'm releasing this intermediate version
to give 0.97 users the benefit of the changes that have been made since.

Still to come:

        - Support for more than 2 users (the tough one!)
        - Support for sending messages from within a chat session
        - Support for receiving DLG Broadcast messages from within a chat session
        - Anything else people want that I consider worthwhile.

For now, source is still not being included -- I will most likely include
it with the final V1.0 release.

0.98g:

- Recompiled with SAS/C 6.3.

- New global setting StayInEdTalk. If non-zero, then new users will stay in
  EdTalk by default when they finish a chat session. If zero, they will be
  returned to the main BBS menu. If unspecified, it defaults to non-zero.
  In any case, this can be changed by each individual user later on if they
  like.

- Added MinAliasLevel to control what users can use aliases

- Fixed a small bug where the QuitChar would only be recognised as a way to
  abort a wait for user if it was typed in upper case

0.98f:

- Added CTRL-O option to full screen chat mode. This will toggle the windows
  between scrolling mode and wrap-around mode without having to return to
  the user options menu.

0.98e:

- Increased maxmium window height to 35 lines to accomodate PAL interlace
  users (with up to 66 lines per screen)

- While waiting for a user to come and chat, the QuitChar is now recognised
  as a valid way to get back to the EdTalk menu (usually 'M' or 'Q')

- Users with a level too low to allow them to use EdTalk are now listed
  on the chat menu as unavailable, rather than not appearing at all.

0.98d:

- Added Alias option to preferences. This name will be used during chat
  sessions, though the user's real name is still used outside of chat

- Each user can now turn on and off CTRl-G's for incoming messages on the
  status line in their user options

- Changed format of each user's EdTalk.prefs file; it's now just a standard
  text file, rather than stored in the filenote.

- EdTalk.Config ScreenChar option has been changed to OptionChar to support
  the new user options menu (which now contains the screen type, among other
  options)

- Optional %ANSI command line option for using colour in a CLI window now
  recognises COLOR as well as ON as a valid keyword.

- Added Scroll mode for high-speed modem users. This uses ANSI commands to
  scroll the screen window, instead of wrapping around the text, which some
  users may find preferable. It can be enabled in the user options. Note that
  this might cause problems with some PC comms packages!

- Fine-tuned some ANSI colours for more consistency

0.98c:

- Increased timeout used to move cursor from remote window back into local
  window to 3 seconds, since many users aren't very quick at typing.

- Fixed bug whereby if you paged a user while waiting for them to come and
  chat, and they chose that moment to enter EdTalk, they weren't asked if
  they wanted to chat with you, but instead were brought straight to the
  EdTalk menu.

0.98b:

- Fixed bug where trying to chat to a user that had never used EdTalk before
  sometimes made user unavailable for chat.

- If user has clear screen turned on in ANSI settings, then the screen will
  be cleared when typing '?' to redisplay the EdTalk menu, and when a user
  gives up waiting for a user to come and chat.

0.98a:

- If you are at the EdTalk menu and someone else tries to chat with you,
  changing your availability status to Unavailable will tell the other
  person that you don't want to chat
--------------
EdTalk V0.97 -- 22 April 1993 (First public release!)

- EdTalk menu now displays "Sending EdTalk message" if a user is using the
  <P>age option while waiting to chat to someone

- If a user tries to send a message using EdTalk -s, and there is someone
  waiting to chat to them already, they are given the opportunity to start
  chatting with that user immediately, just like when they enter EdTalk.

- Changed message telling user's to <P>age the person they want to chat to
  to be displayed in yellow rather than green, to make it stand out.

- Changed message displayed when switching from full screen to line mode to
  be a little clearer (it now says "Switching in 3 seconds ... press any key
  to cancel").

--------------
EdTalk V0.96 -- 19 April 1993

- Line mode now works! If either user has selected line mode, then they
  will both chat using line mode (it's not feasible for one person to be
  in line mode and one person in full screen mode).

  In line mode, users can switch to full screen mode if they both press
  CTRL-F (when the first user types it, the second user gets a message
  saying he should press it too if he wants to use full screen mode).

  In full screen mode, either user can switch to line mode by typing
  CTRL-N. There will be a 3 second delay while the other user is informed
  (to stop them being taken by surprise) and during this delay, the user
  that typed CTRL-N can cancel the request by pressing any key. This helps
  prevent against accidental activation due to line noise.

- There is a new -C command line option that allows you to specify a
  configuration file other than DlgConfig:Misc/EdTalk.prefs. This can be
  useful if you need special control over EdTalk's access. For example, you
  might prefer not to EVER allow users to page the Sysop using EdTalk with
  the -S option. You could setup two config files, the standard one (for use
  with normal EdTalk), and a second one for use with EdTalk -S.

- The CTRL-O option (which cycled the other CTRL options on the top line of
  the screen) has been changed to ESC which is easier to remember. ESC also
  works in line mode.

- When you select a user to chat to from the menu, you now get a message
  telling you if they've been paged automatically (i.e. they're already
  inside EdTalk) or if you should page them (they're elsewhere on the
  system).
--------------
EdTalk V0.95 -- 24 March 1993

Just a minor update to fix some small bugs

- Changed timeout used when switching cursor back from remote window to local
  window from 0.5 seconds to 0.8 seconds which reduces excessive cursor
  activity when chatting with a slow typer.

- Changed input routine used for sending messages to other users to filter
  out possibly problematic sequences. Any % sign is now replaced by / (to
  avoid problems with printf() interpreting them differently) and any quotes
  are replaced by < at the start and > at the end (quotes cause problems
  when passed on the command line to DLG:Broadcast).

- Changed silly bug introduced in 0.94 whereby you can't start chatting with
  a user listed on the menu if that user is unavailable, even if the user
  is currently waiting to chat with you.

- Fixed small bug where long messages broadcast to a user waiting for another
  user to respond to a chat request ended up corrupted and overwritten.
--------------
EdTalk V0.94 -- 19 March 1993

- IMPORTANT CHANGE TO CONFIGURATION FILE. The Ports line now starts with
  a number representing a user level. This allows you to have multiple
  Ports lines, one for each level of user you support. All the ports lines
  should be listed in ascending numerical order for it to work properly.

  The format is like this:

        PORTS = 10,   TR0,TR1,TR2
        PORTS = 20,   TR0,TR1,TR2,TR3
        PORTS = 200,  TR0,TR1,TR2,TR3,TL0,CLI

  In this example, users at level 10 can only see and chat to people on
  ports TR0-TR2. Registered users on level 20 can also see people logged
  onto the special "secret" registered users port, TR3, as well as all
  the people on TR0-TR2. Co-Sysops on level 200 can also see the Sysop
  on TL0, or if he/she runs EdTalk in the CLI.

  In all cases, if a user in one EdTalk session tries to chat to another
  user, the other user will always see the first user listed on the chat
  menu as "Trying to chat to you".

- Activity strings when inside EdTalk are now slightly smarter in taking
  into account the ports the user has access to. If one user's activity
  string is "Chatting with Eddy Carroll" and the user viewing the activity
  doesn't have access to the port that Eddy Carroll is logged into, then
  the activity string will appear as "Chatting with someone" instead.

- Changed the way the Availability indicator works, since it was proving
  a little confusing to some callers (they would see someone listed in
  Who's online but because they were unavailable, they didn't appear in
  the chat menu, and the user's thought EdTalk was broken).

  Now the way it works is that unavailable users are still listed in the
  chat menu, but their name is printed in green, not cyan, and if you try
  and chat to them, it says "Sorry, %s is not available at the moment."

- Small cosmetic fix -- when users enter EdTalk, they are listed as
  "Entering Edtalk" in the activity list, rather than "At EdTalk Menu."
  This looks better if another user is waiting to chat with them, since
  the "At EdTalk menu" string never appear on their screen.

- Some minor cosmetic changes to the colour scheme used for internal
  messages.

- Added -s option to command line. If this is given (EDTALK -s %port %uname)
  then EdTalk will give a list of users online, let the caller choose one
  of them, and then send a message. This is similar to DLG's Broadcast
  utility, but has the advantage that it can also send messages directly
  to people currently using EdTalk. It's designed to be installed as a
  separate menu option to the main EdTalk option, possibly something like
  "[S] Send msg to other node" or something along those lines.

  The list of users displayed is similar to that displayed when at the
  Chat menu in EdTalk, except that in this listing, Unavailable users will
  be listed (most people don't mind getting one line messages from other
  users, even if they're not in the mood for a full chat).

- Changed broadcast routine so that it now lets users send a full 70 chars
  of message to other users if the other user is not currently in EdTalk,
  otherwise lets them send sufficient characters so that the message
  "User name says: xxxxxx" will fit onto a single 80 column line, i.e.
  adjusted to the length of the sender's name.

- Removed MenuSets and CharSets configuration file options. Now, each user
  is asked what kind of graphics they prefer as part of their initial
  questionnaire, and thereafter, can change the bar type while chatting
  by typing CTRL-B.

- Added CTRL-O option in chat mode that cycles the top command line through
  the different available commands (since they no longer fit onto a single
  line).

- Added support for CTRL-G. When someone types CTRL-G, it will flash both
  their screen and the other person's screen

- Modified backspace routine to allow backspacing onto previous line

--------------
EdTalk V0.93 -- 17 March 1993

- Changed exit character in chat mode to <CTRL-Z> from <ESC>. This avoids
  problems with people accidentally using ANSI cursor keys and finding out
  that they're not chatting any more.

- Fixed small bug in CTRL-L refresh that would sometimes draw the place marker
  over the window divider bar.

- Made internal handling of messages much more intelligent. Now, when you
  start waiting to chat with someone, if the other user is in EdTalk,
  they get a message saying "Joe User is waiting to chat with you."

- When you send a page message to someone else, if they are in EdTalk,
  then the message is sent internally and they see it immediately without
  having to exit. If they are not in EdTalk, then the message is sent using
  DLG:Broadcast.

- When you are in chat, any messages received from other people in EdTalk
  (but not chatting with you) will be displayed in the Status line at the
  bottom of the screen, and accompanied by a bell. This makes it easy for
  you to see when other users are trying to get your attention, even while
  they are chatting. The status line is automatically cleared when you have
  pressed RETURN three types with no new messages coming in.

- The activity strings displayed for individual users in the chat menu,
  or when you are waiting to chat to someone, are now intelligent if the
  user happens to already be in EdTalk -- they tell you who the user is
  waiting for, or chatting with.

- Various other tweaks and refinements.

--------------
EdTalk V0.92 -- 16 March 1993

- Added clear window command (CTRL-W) to erase your typing window in full
  screen mode

- Added place marker in window to show you what line you're on -- this is
  a white bar that always appears below the current line in each window so
  you can see at a glance where the last text typed appeared.

- Added broadcast message option (P) to let you send a message to the user
  you are currently waiting for.

- Removed some dead console code, recovering about 6K of space from the
  executable file. Even with the new features, it's still about 4K shorter
  than V0.91.

- Documented a feature which makes EdTalk use Hotkeys on its menu if the
  user has hotkey menus selected.

- Fixed small glitch in printing of activity string while waiting for user
  which would sometimes make it print nothing at all.

- The list of users you can chat to now includes any users who are currently
  trying to chat with you, even if those users would not normally appear on
  the chat list. Users waiting to chat to you have their activity string
  displayed in white.

- If someone else is waiting to chat to you when you enter EdTalk, it will
  now give you the option of chatting with them or not; if you decide not
  to, the other user gets told you have decided not to chat today. This
  question is repeated for every user who is trying to chat to you, so if
  three people try and chat to you at the same time, you can decide which
  one to respond to.

- Added default options to all the Yes/No prompts; the default option is now
  displayed in yellow and is activated if you just press Return on its own.

- Now supports the Available user option. If a user sets this option to
  Unavailable, then they will never appear in the list of available users.

- New command for configuration file: MinUserLevel = <num>. This defines the
  minimum level of any users that will be listed on the chat menu. If you
  don't give it, it defaults to 1 (i.e. everybody). This is useful if you
  don't give new users access to the EdTalk menu option and you don't want
  those users to appear on the Chat menu for others to try and chat to them.

--------------
EdTalk V0.91 -- 15 March 1993

- Initial version, quite a few things not implemented but basically
  functional

--------------
